// T4 self-append: `L = append(L, x)` grows L's object, so L stays releasable.
//
// This shape needed TWO changes and NEITHER alone was worth a byte:
//   1. `append` does not retain its slice argument. Before that, arg 0 was marked
//      escaping and condition 1 refused the local.
//   2. `L = append(L, x)` is not a REBIND. Before that, condition 4 refused it,
//      because it counts bindings and an assignment is normally a second one.
//
// Measured on the way through: RELEASE_NO_ESCAPES, then RELEASE_NO_REBOUND after
// change 1, then RELEASE_OK only with both. It is the daemon's `parts` shape.
//
// WHY THIS IS SAFE. append writes into the existing buffer or reallocs, and
// goo_realloc frees the old base itself, so the intermediate buffers are already
// gone. One local, one live buffer at exit, and ownership never moves.
//
// The dangerous cousin is `t := append(s, x)`, which can leave t.data == s.data
// and give one buffer two owners. It stays refused by condition 2, and
// release_decision_test row 19 pins that.
//
// Grows to 64 elements so the buffer reallocs several times, which exercises the
// realloc path rather than only the first allocation.
//
// IMPORT-FREE, for the reason the other arc probes record.
package main

var sink int

func build(n int) {
	xs := []int{}
	for i := 0; i < 64; i++ {
		xs = append(xs, i+n)
	}
	sink = sink + len(xs)
}

func main() {
	for i := 0; i < 2000; i++ {
		build(i)
	}
}
